/* ==========================================================================
   Koomzo — Functional Specification, as DATA.
   The document renders from this file, so adding a module or refining a screen
   is a data edit, not a layout edit. That is what makes the spec organic.

   Shape of a module entry:
     id, name, icon, tint, status, oneLine
     purpose      : who it is for and what job it does
     entities[]   : { name, key, fields[], notes }
     screens[]    : { name, route, role, device, job, states[], interactions[], reads[], writes[] }
     rules[]      : invariants the backend must enforce regardless of client
     events[]     : { name, when, payload }        — for the event bus / webhooks
     endpoints[]  : { m, path, note }              — the contract the UI expects
     open[]       : unresolved questions
   ========================================================================== */

window.SPEC_META = {
  version: '1.1',
  updated: '28 August 2026',
  owner: 'Design → Backend handoff',
  intro: 'This is the single source of truth for what the Koomzo front end does, so backend services can be built against it without guessing. It is written from the interface inward: every screen states what it reads, what it writes, and which rules must hold even if a client misbehaves. It grows as modules are designed and refined — nothing here is final until its module ships. Hotel and Grocery, added in 0.5, are deliberately thin: neither ships a register or a kitchen of its own. They compose the modules that already exist and add only the objects their trade actually needs. Signage, added in 0.8, is the same argument turned outward: it owns players, loops and layouts, and reads every price, menu line and queue ticket live from the module that already holds it.',
};

