    rules:[
      'A unit may hold at most one in-house stay at a time. Overlapping stays on one unit are rejected at write, not filtered at read.',
      'Availability = sellable units minus off-sale units minus stays overlapping the window. Off sale must subtract from every availability answer, including OTA pushes.',
      'rate_snapshot is written onto the stay at booking. Changing a rate plan never alters an existing stay or a posted folio line.',
      'The folio is append-only. Corrections are credit lines with a reason and an actor.',
      'A stay cannot reach departed while its folio balance is non-zero, unless the balance is transferred to a company account or written off with an owner PIN and a reason.',
      'A deposit is a liability, not revenue. Withholding any part of it requires a reason and an evidence reference, and is reported separately from room revenue.',
      'Charges posted from Restaurant or Retail carry their source module and original document id. The original order remains immutable and settled-to-folio, never deleted.',
      'The night audit is idempotent per site per business date. A second run posts nothing and returns the first run.',
      'A nightly stay accrues one room line per night from the audit. Weekly and monthly tenancies are billed by the tenancy run and must never be double-charged by the audit.',
      'The statutory register cannot be filed with a missing ID. This is a hard block on the audit, not a warning.',
      'Tourist levy is calculated per guest per night from the rate in force on that night, snapshotted onto the line.',
      'Checking out releases the unit to housekeeping as a departure clean. A unit may not return to sellable without passing through clean.',
      'Occupancy, ADR and RevPAR are computed from audit runs, not from live stays, so a report never changes after the day closes.',
      'Hotel owns no register. A drink at reception is a Retail sale and a dinner is a Restaurant order; both reach the guest through the same charge-to-room contract. A property with a shop turns Retail on rather than getting a second till.',
      'Charge-to-room is offered only where the Hotel module is on AND the posting module holds the roomcharge capability AND the room has an in-house stay. Absent any of the three, the tender does not render and the endpoint rejects.',
      'The rail is shaped by the job as well as the capability: a housekeeper sees rooms and their own cleaning list, a technician sees rooms and faults, neither sees folios or rates. This is a presentation of the role rule, not a second permission system.',
      'Attention rules shipped by this module, each toggleable: hotel.arrivals_due (Bookings — arrivals not checked in), hotel.rooms_not_ready (Cleaning — actor-scoped for housekeepers, property-scoped for the desk), hotel.faults_open (Cleaning — technicians), hotel.departures_owing (Folios — departures with a balance), hotel.audit_exceptions (Audit — blocking severity: a missing ID stops the day closing), hotel.not_clocked_in (Roster — rostered staff past their shift start).',
      'Capability and plan placement for this module: Lite — reservations, guest records, housekeeping, staff roster (free plan). Mid — folio, rate plans, maintenance, shift board, charge to room from F&B, OTA bookings, statutory register (starter plan). Full — deposits, long stays, night audit, occupancy reporting (standard plan) and channel sync (pro plan). A capability above the plan ceiling renders visible but locked, with a route to billing; it never silently disappears.',
      'The roster is one row per person per date, never an array on the user record. A week is a date-range query, so a mid-week hire, a leaver and a swap all fall out of the same model.',
      'A person holds exactly one shift code per date. A double shift is modelled as a shift definition, not as two roster rows.',
      'Coverage requirements advise, they never block: the shift board flags a short post and offers cover, but a manager may always save an under-covered day. Trade is never stopped by a roster rule.',
      'Late is derived at read time from attendance against the shift start; it is never stored as a status, so a late clock-in corrects the display retroactively.',
      'Publishing a week is an event, not a flag on rows: staff see the last published version while a manager edits the next draft.',
      'Colour on Rooms is a signal, not decoration: a unit card is white with a hairline border and one status dot; state is carried by the dot plus its label, and washes or coloured card borders are not used. Coral is reserved for shortfalls and faults across the roster and shift screens.',
    ],
